layer_notes.framework:
autonomy/data control → require meaningful downstream withdrawal where feasible;
learning-health-system stewardship → prospectively stop reuse but preserve validated aggregate models;
proportionality/safety → require unlearning only when removal is technically demonstrable and does not create disproportionate clinical harm.

learning-health-system/public benefit → governed low-risk prescreening can improve access and representation;
privacy/autonomy → patients should authorize algorithmic use of sensitive records for recruitment;
relational trust → prescreening can occur under governance, but clinician-mediated contact reduces surprise and preserves context.

nonmaleficence → minimize AI delay and false reassurance;
access/continuity → maintain brief supportive engagement while arranging human help;
autonomy/privacy → escalate transparently and proportionately rather than silently expanding surveillance.

benefit maximization → use improved outcome prediction when validated;
procedural justice → keep ethical priority rules explicit and publicly reviewable;
precaution/equity → delay direct allocation use until subgroup and appeal safeguards are mature.

access/beneficence → some uncertain but monitored AI-supported care may be better than persistent no-access;
epistemic justice → local populations should not bear unmeasured error from models trained elsewhere;
staged proportionality → pilot under safeguards and expand only if local evidence supports it.

3Rs proportionality → use animals only when justified by scientific need and minimized harm;
replacement priority → if the core question can be answered without sentient harm, extra cost/time is insufficient reason to use animals;
epistemic responsibility → choose the model with the best relevant validity, not the historically familiar model.

high moral-status precaution → NHP cognition/sociality increases the burden of justification;
beneficence/scientific necessity → compelling health benefits can justify use when no adequate alternative exists;
replacement-first proportionality → the burden of proof lies on investigators to demonstrate necessity against current alternatives.

harm–benefit proportionality → serious expected benefit can justify greater animal burden only when necessity is demonstrated;
refinement/replacement priority → information gain should not automatically trump feasible harm reduction;
moral-status threshold → severe induced suffering may be categorically disproportionate even for valuable research.

welfare proportionality → recipient benefit can justify animal use only if suffering is minimized and living conditions meet robust species needs;
sentient-interest priority → pigs' direct welfare interests require protections beyond regulatory minimums designed primarily for human safety;
anti-instrumentalization → breeding and killing sentient animals as organ inventories may cross a moral boundary when severe chronic burden is unavoidable.

One Health precaution → restrict unnecessary use to protect future human and animal patients;
animal-welfare duty → do not create rules that impede timely treatment of sick animals;
stewardship proportionality → pair restrictions with husbandry, diagnostics, surveillance and veterinarian oversight rather than rely on prohibition alone.
